om/en/places/pun-thao/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/pun-thao/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/pun-thao/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</h1><p class="english">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">5 verified items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="pun-thao">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/pun-thao.webp" alt="ศาลเจ้าปุนเถ่ากงอ๊า" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8313123, 98.3684438 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8313123,98.3684438" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://goo.gl/maps/JREsvAnLQ2vycGG78" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A shrine from the Chinese community in a mining port</h3><p class="era-years">The construction year is unknown; it was probably built during the formation of Takua Pa's old-town community, but the year cannot yet be specified</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Official evidence from Takua Pa Municipality identifies Bun Thao Kong A Shrine as the town's first shrine and places it in the old port district known as Chap Se or Chap Sao. This locates the shrine within Takua Pa's history of waterborne trade and old-market activity, but the documents found give neither a construction start year nor the name of an individual founder</p><p>The Talat Yai Community database identifies the builders as Chinese immigrants who came to Takua Pa to mine, mentioning groups such as Hokkien, Hainanese, and Cantonese. This presents the shrine as both a ritual site and an anchor for the workers and merchants of a mining town. However, there is not enough direct evidence to determine which group founded it or what specific role the shrine played in the port system</p><p>The municipality also records an incense burner “presented by King Rama VI”. This is evidence of a past relationship between the local shrine and the Siamese state, but the year, occasion, and provenance of the object are not specified</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan Fiscal Year 2020 (2563 BE)</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>An old shrine with gaps in its transition records</h3><p class="era-years">The year of restoration or any ownership change is unknown; activity records exist from at least 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Among the verifiable public sources, no record has been found that the shrine was abandoned, changed hands, or restored in any particular year, and no ancient-monument registration announcement has been found for this shrine. Such details should therefore not be inferred from the context of the old town or other shrines</p><p>What can be verified is that in 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564), a video recorded the Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin or Pae Kong at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine in Chap Se, while another video mentions the oil-bathing ceremony, Phra La ceremony, and bringing in the sacred fire. This shows that the shrine was still a ritual space for Takua Pa descendants at that time</p><p>As for whether the 2004 tsunami (พ.ศ. 2547) affected the building, and the effects of tin-mine closures and economic change in the town, no source has been found that addresses this shrine directly. This is recorded as a research gap, without concluding whether damage occurred</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai: Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin, Pae Kong, at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai: Oil-bathing and Phra La ceremonies at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine</a> · <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fine Arts Department, Ancient Monuments Database</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Still alive as a community shrine, but 2026 status data is incomplete</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>The latest evidence found does not verify opening hours, an official custodian, an admission fee, or the building's condition in 2026 (พ.ศ. 2569). It should therefore not be written that the shrine is open to tourists every day. What can be verified is that it is on Udom Thara Road in Talat Yai Community and has a history connected to the old port district of Chap Se</p><p>In terms of community use, a 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564) video shows Chinese ceremonies taking place and Takua Pa descendants returning to join the activities. The shrine therefore still appears to be a place of faith and shared community memory, but there is not enough public evidence to identify an annual event calendar or a management committee</p><p>No news from the past five years has been found confirming a restoration project, award, or development project specifically for this shrine, and no current image from an official agency with verifiable usage rights is available</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai (2021 activity video)</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">21 September 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564)</p><h3>Birthday ceremony and faith activities at the shrine</h3><p>There is a video recording the birthday ceremony of Hok Tek Chia Sin / Pae Kong at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine, Chap Se.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">2021 (2564 BE)</p><h3>Oil bathing ceremony, Phra La, and inviting the sacred fire</h3><p>Another video mentions the shrine's rituals and the return of Takua Pa descendants to join the event.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Start of shrine construction</h3><p>The construction year and founder's name are not yet found; official sources identify it as the first shrine in Takua Pa town.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Incense burner bestowed by King Rama VI</h3><p>The municipality states there is an incense burner bestowed by King Rama VI, but does not specify the year or occasion.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Incense burner linked to King Rama VI</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Municipal documents state that there is a royally bestowed incense burner inside the shrine. There is no verified information on its display location or public viewing availability today.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Principal deity Pun Thao Kong</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A travel article states that the deity Pun Thao Kong is the principal deity inside. This information is not verified by the shrine or official agencies.</p><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Chap Se shophouses on Udom Thara Road</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A viewing spot in the area around the shrine, not inside it: The community database mentions two-story old buildings on Udom Thara Road in the Chap Se district.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2021-10-28 · PaiPaPai</p><h3>Birthday of Hok Tek Chia Sin (Pae Kong), Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine, Chap Se, Takua Pa town</h3><p>A news video/recording of the shrine's birthday ceremony activities.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Takua Pa Town Municipality states that this shrine is the first shrine of Takua Pa town.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Inside the shrine is an incense burner that municipal documents state was bestowed by King Rama VI.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Chap Se area shophouses, Udom Thara Road <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Old town wall camp, Udom Thara Road <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/rong-jae/">The Shrine in the Garden</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Municipal documents state that Bun Thao Kong A Shrine was the first shrine in Takuapa and is located in the old port district known as Chap Se or Chap Sao. The municipality records that the shrine contains an incense burner presented by King Rama VI, but does not specify the year or occasion of the presentation.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>A travel article states that the shrine's principal deity is Pun Thao Kong. This has not been verified by the shrine or an official agency. A sight in the area around the shrine, not inside it: the community database mentions a two-storey old building on Udom Thara Road in Chap Se</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>Documents mention the shrine in Takua Pa's old port district</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Municipality, Environmental Action Plan for fiscal year 2020 (พ.ศ. 2563), document pages 11 and 13</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre, Talat Yai Community Database</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dash MV, Phang Nga and Takua Pa destinations, cultural attractions</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Google Maps, the shrine pin linked from the Dash MV article</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://goo.gl/maps/JREsvAnLQ2vycGG78" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan Fiscal Year 2020 (2563 BE)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dash MV, cultural tourism article</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">PaiPaPai: Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin, Pae Kong</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">PaiPaPai: Oil-bathing and Phra La ceremonies</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The main Thai and English names are according to the brief. No readable sources have been found using the exact English name; alternative names cite municipal documents and a travel article, while SEO keywords are suggestions for searching, not evidence of official names.</li><li>Coordinates were read from a Google Maps redirect link attached to an article. The destination uses the name Bun Tao Kong Shrine. The destination can be opened, but there is no independent second map or confirming sign photo, so confidence is medium.</li><li>The road and community are cited from the religious sites list in municipal documents, while the subdistrict is cited from the Talat Yai page of the Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre. No specific building number or postal code was found.</li><li>No opening hours and admission fees specific to this shrine were found, so it is not concluded that it is open every day or free to enter.</li><li>No phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE verified as a direct channel for this shrine was found.</li><li>No directions to the shrine from Takua Pa town center, Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport with verifiable distances were found.</li><li>No construction year, individual founder name, restoration year, or evidence of specific roles in the tin industry outside of being founded by Chinese miners was found.</li><li>No evidence directly classifying the architectural style of the shrine building was found, so the Sino-Portuguese description of buildings in the community is not used for the shrine.</li><li>No ancient monument registration announcement specifying this shrine directly was found.</li><li>No suitable visiting times, duration, dress codes, parking, restrooms, ramps, or accessibility information for the elderly and wheelchairs were found.</li><li>There is not yet enough information on things to see inside the shrine to complete 3-6 items whose current condition can be verified, so two items are given based on source descriptions and one item in the area around the shrine.</li><li>Search results found a video of the Sae Yid ceremony for Hok Tek Chia Sin dated September 21, 2021, but the page could not be opened, so it is not used to confirm the festival, month, or lunar calendar.</li><li>Many search results for the Vegetarian Festival relating to the Bun Tao Kong Shrine in Khuekkhak Subdistrict or the Guan Yu Shrine cannot be used as a schedule for the Chap Se district shrine.</li><li>The nearby location has evidence that it is on Udom Thara Road in the same district, but actual walking routes and times have not been verified.</li><li>No official image link clearly identifying this shrine with usage rights was found.</li><li>The municipal plan cited is the Environmental Operations Plan for Fiscal Year 2020, not the original brochure. Historical text in the community database is similar to the municipal document, so they are not considered two independent sources.</li><li>The exact construction year, individual founder names, and specific reasons for construction have not found evidence.</li><li>No restoration year, change of hands, abandonment, or ancient monument registration has been found yet.</li><li>No evidence has been found yet on how the shrine was affected by the 2004 tsunami or the closure of the tin mines.</li><li>Caretakers, opening hours, admission fees, building conditions, and annual activities in 2026 are not verified.</li><li>There are no verifiable old photos from archives or local historical sources published online.</li><li>Current photos found are sample images from video covers, not image files from official agencies, and require permission before use.</li><li>No news from the past five years regarding specific restoration projects, awards, or development projects for the shrine has been found beyond the 2021 activity videos.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\rest\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Where to Eat and Rest in Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Restaurants, cafes and snack stops in Takua Pa that the guide author has visited, with real opening hours and map links, plus a printable walking leaflet."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Where to Eat and Rest in Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="Restaurants, cafes and snack stops in Takua Pa that the guide author has visited, with real opening hours and map links, plus a printable walking leaflet."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/rest/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Takua Pa food, cafés, and places to stop", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/rest/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/rest/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/" aria-current="page">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/rest/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="intro"><a class="eyebrow" href="/en/">Takua Pa 101 / Old Town Guide</a><h1>Take a break, eat and explore Takuapa</h1><p class="lead">Shops and stops personally visited by the people who made this guide, with opening hours and directions</p></div><p>The whole old town is walkable, but there is little shade and the afternoon sun is strong. Stops along the way make it easier to cover the area.</p><p class="small">The list below is selected from<a href="https://siwaracafe.com/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Siwara Cafe's Takua Pa food and travel guide</a> The people who made this website · Unlike the list of shops on<a href="/en/eat/">the Food and Souvenirs page</a> Every shop listed here has been visited in person, so the opening hours and coordinates are practical and current · Even so, small shops can change their hours at any time, so call ahead before making a long trip.</p><h2>Restaurants and food</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Baan%20Lapp%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-baan-lapp.webp" alt="BAAN LAPP บ้านลัพพ์ (โฮมมี่ &amp; สเต๊ก ตะกั่วป่า)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>BAAN LAPP (Homey &amp; Steak, Takua Pa)</h3><p>A warm, homey restaurant and cafe with a Hidden Gem style, serving steak, pasta, and carefully prepared fusion dishes.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 11:00 - 21:00 (closed Wednesdays) · ~2.2 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">From 17:30 onward, the lights create a pleasant atmosphere. It is suitable for a family dinner.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Baan%20Lapp%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-moo-satay-kruaew.webp" alt="ขนมจีนหมูสะเต๊ะครูแอ๋ว (ทุ่งพระโพธิ์)" width="1193" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Kru Aew's Khanom Chin and Pork Satay (Thung Phra Pho)</h3><p>A tucked-away shop in Thung Phra Pho serving tender, juicy charcoal-grilled pork satay marinated with spices, hot off the grill with boldly flavored southern Thai khanom chin and curry.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 12:00 - 18:00 (closed Thursdays) · ~2.4 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The pork satay is grilled fresh, skewer by skewer. A large set of 20 to 30 skewers is recommended with the khanom chin.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-mee-talad-khwang.webp" alt="บะหมี่พังงา ตลาดขวาง" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Phang Nga Noodles, Khwang Market</h3><p>Springy, tender handmade egg noodles made with an old Hokkien recipe, tossed with fragrant fried garlic oil and large pieces of crispy pork lard, for a balanced, mellow flavor.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 09:00 - 15:30 (closed Tuesdays) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Try the "special dry tom yum noodles with crispy pork lard". Toss well and enjoy without adding seasoning.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AE%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%99" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-pa-da-hokkien.webp" alt="ป้าดา หมี่ฮกเกี้ยน (ถนนศรีตะกั่วป่า)" width="1200" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Pa Da Hokkien Noodles (Thanon Si Takua Pa)</h3><p>A long-running Hokkien noodle shop on Thanon Si Takua Pa. The springy noodles are stir-fried with a fragrant wok-charred aroma, plenty of seafood, and a runny egg.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 07:00 - 17:00 (daily) · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Try the "Hokkien noodles stir-fried with a runny egg". Break the yolk and mix it into the hot noodles for a rich, savory taste.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AE%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%99" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-bang-deen.webp" alt="ร้านบังดีน ข้าวหมกไก่ &amp; โรตีแกง (Michelin Guide)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Bangdeen Chicken Biryani &amp; Curry Roti (Michelin Guide)</h3><p>A well-known Muslim breakfast restaurant recognized by the Michelin Guide. Fragrant, tender spiced chicken biryani is served with a bold dipping sauce, along with soft roti and chicken massaman curry.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 06:00 - 13:30 (daily) · ~2.8 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The roti dough is freshly kneaded and fried until crisp yet soft. Try the massaman curry roti with hot pulled tea.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B3%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B5" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-pu-dam.webp" alt="ร้านปูดำ (บางนายสี)" width="1010" height="761" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Black Crab Restaurant (Bang Nai Si)</h3><p>A tucked-away restaurant beside a mangrove canal in Bang Nai Si, serving fresh wild black crab with firm, intensely sweet meat and boldly seasoned local-style dishes.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 10:00 - 21:00 (daily) · ~3.2 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Steamed black crab and crab fried rice are the most frequently ordered dishes. Call ahead to check fresh supplies or go before evening.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B3%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%9C%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-kae-padthai.webp" alt="เก๋ ผัดไทย (ตลาดเก่าตะกั่วป่า)" width="848" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Kae Pad Thai (Takua Pa Old Market)</h3><p>A popular Pad Thai shop in the old town area. Chewy noodles stir-fried in a rich traditional tamarind sauce, with large, fresh, and bouncy shrimp.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 16:30 to 21:30 (daily) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Fresh shrimp Pad Thai wrapped in egg is the specialty. Squeeze fresh lime over it and eat with banana blossom and pennywort. Flavorful without extra seasoning.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%9C%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%A1" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-jae-ouan.webp" alt="เจ๊อ้วน ทะเลสด (บ้านน้ำเค็ม)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Jae Uan Talay Sod (Baan Nam Kem)</h3><p>A renowned seafood restaurant in the Baan Nam Kem area. Fresh ingredients delivered directly from fishing boats daily. Spicy seafood dip made with real lime. Evening seaside atmosphere.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 10:30 to 21:30 (Daily) · ~11.5 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Great for dinner. Large tiger prawns with firm, sweet meat paired with spicy seafood dip.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%A1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Stops and Attractions</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-old-town-street.webp" alt="ถนนวัฒนธรรมศรีตะกั่วป่า &amp; สตรีทอาร์ตตึกโบราณ" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Thanon Si Takua Pa &amp; Old Building Street Art</h3><p>An old shophouse district with arched walkways (Five-Foot Way) featuring street art that tells the stories of the Baba people and the town's tin mining history.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Open all day (09:00 to 17:00 recommended) · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">You can easily walk through various alleys connecting to Tao Sor pastry shops and old town eateries.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-tam-nang.webp" alt="น้ำตกตำหนัง (อุทยานแห่งชาติศรีพังงา)" width="1200" height="795" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Tam Nang Waterfall (Si Phang Nga National Park)</h3><p>A large waterfall in the middle of a lush rainforest in Si Phang-nga National Park. The water cascades down a 63-meter high cliff into a clear pool where Mahseer barbs swim.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 08:30 to 16:30 (Daily) · ~21.8 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Between 10:00 and 14:00, the sunlight hits the water surface beautifully. There is a shaded resting spot under large trees.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%81%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-iron-bridge.webp" alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง (สะพานโคกขนุน)" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Boonsung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun Bridge)</h3><p>A historical landmark from the prosperous tin mining era. Built from the steel parts of an old tin dredger, elegantly spanning the Takua Pa River.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Open 24 hours (16:30 to 18:30 recommended) · ~2.3 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Between 17:00 and 18:30, the sunset light shines through the steel bridge structure, turning it into a beautiful orange. Great for taking photos.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%81%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%8D%E0%B9%88%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-lad-yai.webp" alt="หลาดใหญ่ ถนนคนเดินสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่า" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Lad Yai, the old town cultural walking street</h3><p>A classic historical walking street. Thanon Si Takua Pa is closed to traffic so locals can set up stalls selling traditional food, local snacks, and crafts under Sino-Portuguese style lighting.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Sundays only 15:30 to 20:30 · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Open only on Sundays. Arriving around 17:00 is recommended when the orange sunlight beautifully lights up the classic old buildings for photos.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%8D%E0%B9%88%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Souvenirs</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%8C" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-tuangrat-taosor.webp" alt="เต้าส้อตวงรัตน์ (ต้นตำรับ 100 ปี)" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Tao Sor Tuang Rat (100-year-old original recipe)</h3><p>A top souvenir of Takua Pa. Thin crispy crust with dense mung bean filling, scented with traditional candle smoke. The recipe has been passed down through generations.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 08:00 to 17:00 (Daily) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The salted egg yolk filling is their signature. Best bought fresh out of the oven. Can be kept for 7 to 10 days.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%8C" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Walking Pamphlet</h2><div class="leaflet-block"><a class="leaflet-art" href="/assets/leaflet/takuapa-walk-leaflet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/leaflet/leaflet-page-1.webp" alt="แผ่นพับเดินเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า หน้าแรก" width="1400" height="990" loading="lazy"></a><div class="leaflet-copy"><p>A two-page pamphlet detailing the wooden house and four stops on the route above. Print on A4 paper, fold in half, and carry it with you. No need to look at your phone in the sun.</p><div class="leaflet-actions"><a class="button" href="/assets/leaflet/takuapa-walk-leaflet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download brochure PDF · 7.6 MB</a><a class="text-link" href="/en/leaflet/">Read on the website instead ↗</a></div><p class="small">Created by<a href="https://siwaracafe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Siwara Cafe</a> Creators of this guide · Free to distribute and print</p></div></div>
site\en\rest\index.html:7:    <h2>Short Walking Route from the Wooden House</h2><div class="short-walk"><article class="walk-point"><a href="/en/places/khun-in/"><img src="/assets/khun-in.webp" alt="บ้านขุนอินทร์" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><div><h3><a href="/en/places/khun-in/">Khun In House</a></h3><p>Notice: Porch Roof and Openings</p><button data-trip-add="khun-in">Add to trip</button></div></article><article class="walk-point"><a href="/en/places/tao-ming/"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" alt="โรงเรียนเต้าหมิง" width="1000" height="667" loading="lazy"></a><div><h3><a href="/en/places/tao-ming/">Tao Ming School</a></h3><p>Notice: Gable and Yellow Balcony</p><button data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></article><article class="walk-point"><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/"><img src="/assets/guan-yu.webp" alt="ศาลเจ้าพ่อกวนอู ซิ่นไช่ตึ๋ง" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><div><h3><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></h3><p>Notice: Door and Red Details</p><button data-trip-add="guan-yu">Add to trip</button></div></article><article class="walk-point"><a href="/en/places/iron-bridge/"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><div><h3><a href="/en/places/iron-bridge/">Boon Soong Iron Bridge</a></h3><p>Notice: Steel Structure and Riverside Landscape</p><button data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></article></div><p>Plan further: <a href="/en/trip/">Plan your trip ↗</a> · <a href="/en/map/">View town map ↗</a></p><aside class="source"><h2>Data Sources</h2><p>Compiled from<a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takua Pa Town Municipality brochure</a> · Data extracted September 6, 2026 (2569 BE). The document does not specify a publication date. Opening hours, shop status, and current schedules are not verified. Please check with the venue before traveling.</p></aside><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Taking a Break While Exploring the Old Town", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/rest/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "ItemList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Baan Lapp (Homie & Steak Takua Pa)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=Baan%20Lapp%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Khru Aew Rice Noodles & Pork Satay (Thung Phra Pho)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%A7"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Phang Nga Noodles Talat Khwang", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 4, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Pa Da Hokkien Noodles (Thanon Si Takua Pa)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AE%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%99"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 5, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Bang Deen Chicken Biryani & Roti Curry (Michelin Guide)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 6, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Pu Dam Restaurant (Bang Nai Si)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B3%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B5"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 7, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Kae Pad Thai (Takua Pa Old Market)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%9C%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 8, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Jae Uan Talay Sod (Baan Nam Kem)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%A1"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 9, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Thanon Si Takua Pa & Old Building Street Art", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 10, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Namtok Tam Nang (Si Phang-nga National Park)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 11, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Saphan Lek Bun Sung (Khok Khanun Bridge)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%81%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 12, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Lard Yai, Old Town Cultural Walking Street", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%8D%E0%B9%88%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2"}}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 13, "item": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Tao Sor Tuang Rat (100-Year Original Recipe)", "url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%8C"}}]}]</script></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
site\en\stories\kuapapoh\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened"><meta property="og:description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="ตะกั่วป่า 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Kuapa Poh, an Old Takua Pa House Brought Back to Life", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>City topics</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · Town Topics</span><h1>Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</h1><p class="lead">A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ · แบรนด์ของโครงการเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>Imagine strolling along Sri Takua Pa Road, a street lined with old shophouses that have stood the test of time. Many of these buildings have their wooden doors quietly shut, sleeping away alongside the long-gone golden age of tin mining. Underneath that quietness, however, lie countless memories of the town, its people, and its trade, waiting to be told.</p><p>In recent years, a creative space project called 'Kuapapoh Ji Show Mueang' has stepped in to open the doors of these once-abandoned old houses and bring them back to life. They did not just repaint or repair the roofs, but are bringing the stories of Takua Pa back on display for the newer generations to see.</p><p>This movement reflects a town trying to stand up and tell its own story through the perspectives of locals, artists, and community collaboration. However, in reading these changes, we must still distinguish between newly created narratives for tourism and historical facts that still need careful verification.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-109.webp" width="400" height="533" alt="ตึกแถวเลขที่ 109 บนถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>We often remember Takua Pa through its prosperous tin mining era. When that industry declined, many buildings were left behind. One of them is house number 78, formerly a mine owner's residence and a tin mining company office. Today, this once-empty space has been transformed into the 'Kuapa Mueang Museum', exhibiting mining equipment, old maps, and important documents for visitors to experience the traces of the past.</p><p>Not far away on the same route is house number 109, the building of the Kang Tang Yui Kuan or Wui Kun shrine, an association of the Hakka Chinese. The second floor of the building still houses the deity Tham Kung Ya for people to pay their respects. But the ground floor space, which used to be empty and unused, has been turned into the 'Kuapapoh Gallery' to host exhibitions and showcase the wisdom of the Takua Pa old town residents.</p><p>Another significant building that the project has dusted off is building number 305, or 'Tao Ming School' (the town's first Chinese school), which was an educational center during the prosperous mining era. The team came up with the idea to revive this space under the name 'Tao Ming: The Spark of Learning', using it as a venue to exhibit knowledge on architecture, culture, and traditions related to Takua Pa.</p><p>The effort to bring these three spaces back into use is an initiative filled with the project owners' determination to add economic value and create worth for the town. We must collectively understand, however, that this is an explanation and operation at the community project level, not an official declaration of historical site status or historical endorsement from government agencies.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Working with community narratives is about balancing beautiful memories with facts that must be verified.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" width="1477" height="1108" alt="อาคารโรงเรียนเต้าหมิง ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>As soon as you hear the name 'Kuapapoh', the first question that usually follows is where this word comes from. The project's archival information explains that this was the term Hokkien Chinese used in the past to call Takua Pa town, dividing the translation into two parts: 'Kuapa' meaning Takua Pa, and 'Poh' meaning market or port.</p><p>When combined, the word conveys 'Takua Pa port market', reflecting the past image of Chinese people sailing across the sea to trade and mine tin, using the pier as a center for exchanging both goods and culture. In addition, in the community's belief, the word Kuapapoh is also embedded in the rituals performed at various shrines during the vegetarian festival, becoming a word with deep meaning in the hearts of the townspeople.</p><p>While this is a beautiful story that connects well with the community's roots, in academic circles, the origin of the Takua Pa town name is still debated with several suggested, not settled hypotheses. Some refer to the ancient name Takola, while others base it on the words takua (lead) and pa (forest).</p><p>Therefore, the project's explanation is considered a record of memory and interpretation through the context of the Hokkien Chinese community, rather than a single, settled historical academic conclusion. This is the reason we must read this story with an open mind and caution.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">not settled academically</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="โรงเรียนเต้าหมิง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The liveliness of the old town district reappeared concretely in September 2026 (2569 BE) when 78 Studio on Takua Pa Culture Street opened its space to host a solo art exhibition titled 'Peranakan Charm · Still Becoming'.</p><p>The artist behind these works is Tinnaphop Ngansathir, or Chang Pong. He is a native of Takua Pa old market, growing up with cartoon drawings, superheroes, and Chinese deities familiar in the local culture. After accumulating artistic experience for a long time, he chose to use his brush tip to convey the stories of the town he is attached to.</p><p>The artworks in the exhibition focus on extracting Peranakan colors, patterns, and clothing for display, serving as a bridge connecting the glorious past with people in the present era. Each painting is not just visually beautiful, but an emotional record of the artist towards the town.</p><p>Even though the project documents clearly record the exhibition hosting and the opening of space for artists to express themselves, what we still do not see in the reports are the number of attendees or a formal economic impact assessment. These activities thus serve as cultural ripples rather than official government statistics.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันที่ยังเห็นได้ในเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><p>To help visitors understand the identity of Takua Pa old town more deeply, the project did not stop at organizing exhibitions. They also compiled and presented the '6 Or' concept to use as a framework for viewing the town's heritage hidden in daily life.</p><p>This concept starts with 'Aphon' (Apparel) or local clothing, whether it is lace fabrics, batik sarongs, and various accessories. Followed by 'Asai' (Abode) which covers the way of life, trading, and living in accordance with the times. As for 'Ahan' (Food), it is indispensable. It is the traditional flavors passed down from generation to generation, and the old restaurants that still keep their stoves on to this day.</p><p>In the dimension of the mind, the town is driven by 'Arom' (Emotion) expressed through art, music, and the sound of drums in the vegetarian festival. Meanwhile, 'Aram' (Temples and Shrines) is the center of faith, including temples, shrines, and Yui Kuan scattered around the old town district. And finally, 'Akhan' (Architecture), the structures that stand as witnesses to change.</p><p>All these concepts were also further developed in the design of souvenirs, such as the Poh Shop project's t-shirts. The revenue from the sales is brought back to fund the restoration and drive the town's activities forward, truly circulating value back to the community.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Kuapapoh project allows us to see the determination of the new generation and locals who want to preserve and build upon the stories of Takua Pa. They created creative spaces, brought in art as a medium, and turned once-closed houses into new travel destinations.</p><p>What is recorded on the project's website and documents serves as a contemporary archive that the community chose to tell about itself. It is a memory full of life and the pride of the residents.</p><p>At the same time, however, reading these stories also calls for us to maintain a balance between appreciation and historical facts. Many parts of the information are explanations that have not been endorsed by government agencies and cannot replace calendars or academic documents.</p><p>This is the challenge and fun of walking around Takua Pa old town today. We have the task of listening to those beautiful stories while simultaneously asking questions and finding answers from reliable sources, so that our journey does not end with just looking at pictures, but truly understanding the town in all dimensions.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The number of attendees or the official economic impact generated by the project</li><li>The legal status or endorsement from government agencies of the three houses</li><li>The clear and settled origin of the Takua Pa town name in history</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Kuapapoh · Project Archives</cite> · Kuapapoh Project · content.json · origin, events, openings sections · <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Defining houses number 78, 109, and 305 as creative spaces</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is an explanation from the project owner, not a declaration of historical site or space status from a government agency. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The origin of the word Kuapapoh translates to Takua Pa port market</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The origin of the Takua Pa town name in historical work still has several suggested, not settled hypotheses, thus the project's explanation should not be used as a settled academic conclusion. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The hosting of the Peranakan Charm exhibition by Tinnaphop Ngansathir in September 2026 (2569 BE)</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Referenced from project data, but not a retrospective official calendar confirming attendee numbers or economic impact. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><section class="research-section" id="project-gallery"><h2>Images from Kuapapoh Project Archive</h2><div data-gallery><div class="gallery-tabs" hidden><button type="button" data-tab="today">Present photos</button><button type="button" data-tab="archive">Historical photos</button></div><div id="gallery-today" data-panel="today"><h3>Present photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div><div id="gallery-archive" data-panel="archive"><h3>Historical photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" width="1078" height="1519" alt="แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh History Board<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · หวุ่ยกุ่น"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" width="1086" height="1448" alt="หวุ่ยกุ่น" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Wui Kun<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" width="900" height="1272" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 1<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" width="900" height="1268" alt="เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 2<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section></main><foo